```

or pin it in `requirements.yml`:

```yaml
collections:
  - name: {{ namespace }}.{{ name }}
    version: "{{ version }}"
```

Requires ansible-core {{ requires_ansible }}.

## Development

Provision a non-editable devkit toolchain outside this checkout, with Black
available for Python formatting, and put its `bin` directory on `PATH`.
After the first checkout (or `git init`), run setup once:

```sh
sh bootstrap.sh                 # automatically runs make hooks-install
```

Setup points Git at the installed devkit's own hooks, never code in a tracked
`.githooks/` directory. The toolchain must be mounted read-only in agent
sandboxes. Host-side Git against an agent clone must always use
`git -c core.hooksPath=/dev/null ...`; the gates belong inside the sandbox and CI.

```sh
# from a checkout at ansible_collections/{{ namespace }}/{{ name }}
ansible-test sanity --docker
ansible-test units --docker
ansible-galaxy collection build
devkit conform                  # the repository standard (ansible-devkit)
devkit check all                # the static checks, configured by devkit.toml
devkit hooks run pre-commit --staged  # the same staged gate, without committing
devkit hooks run pre-push        # the full gate, without pushing
```

The manual gates also work with read-only `.git` (pre-commit checks the
existing index). Hooks require no network, never install missing tools, and
fail if a required tool is absent. Pre-push defaults to `make lint`; customize
`[hooks] pre_push` in `devkit.toml` for a different offline full gate.
`--no-verify` cannot be prevented by Git hooks: CI is the real backstop.

CI, the disclosure gate and releases are the shared workflows of
[ansible-devkit](https://github.com/Richard-Lynch/ansible-devkit), called from
`.github/workflows/` at tag `{{ devkit_ref }}`. A release is a merged pull
request that bumps `version` in `galaxy.yml` and adds the matching
`CHANGELOG.md` section. `export.manifest` lists every tracked path; update it in
the same commit as any file added or removed.

